\details{
\strong{Folds.} Items are dealt to \code{folds} folds under \code{seed}. Without
\code{cluster}, the deal is stratified by label. With \code{cluster} (a document id,
a source), whole clusters are dealt, so items that share a cluster are never
split between training and test; with fewer clusters than \code{folds}, there is
one fold per cluster. For each fold, the vocabulary and idf weights are
fitted on the training texts only (\code{\link[=hr_tfidf]{hr_tfidf()}} with \code{min_df} and
\code{max_features}) and applied to the held-out texts, so no word statistics
from a test text reach its model.

\strong{Model.} For two labels, one logistic regression of the second level
against the first. Its coefficients maximise
sum_i log L_i - (lambda / 2) * sum_j beta_j^2, with the intercept not
penalised, fitted by iteratively reweighted least squares (each step a
weighted ridge solve on the working response; Hastie, Tibshirani and
Friedman 2009, section 4.4) with step halving. \code{lambda = 1} is the same
penalty as scikit-learn's \code{LogisticRegression} default (\code{C = 1}). For more
than two labels, one model per label against the rest, with the
probabilities divided by their sum; a label missing from a training fold
gets probability 0 there. The predicted label is the most probable one,
the first in level order on a tie.

\strong{Accuracy.} The share of items whose predicted label equals the given
one, with a Wilson interval from \code{\link[=hr_prop_ci]{hr_prop_ci()}}. The interval treats items
as independent; with \code{cluster}, read it as too narrow when clusters are
large, and use the out-of-fold predictions in a benchmark report for a
clustered interval.
